more now on strikes this winter and  government plans to keep essential  Services moving 1200 members of the  armed forces are being deployed to drive  ambulances and staff border posts to  tackle strikes this winter we'll be join  now by the chancellor of the dusty of  Lancaster Oliver Dalton Mr Dowden thank  you so much for joining us on Sky News  good morning good morning  I want to start with the nurse's strike  it's the first in NHS history it's a  good place to start I suppose is there  any wriggle room when it comes to pay  the NHS is a special case one could put  it to you  um nurses are using food banks are  unable to finish shifts because they  can't hand over to anyone because  there's no one to hand over to do you  not think there should be an adjustment  from the government and there should be  a resolution to their pay  well you're right that the NHS is a  special case that's why for example last  year when public sector pay was frozen  across the board and we had gave a three  percent pay rise for for all those  across the NHS and that's why this year  we gave it to the independent pay review  body and they came up with this number  which for the lowest paid across the NHS  in fact everyone got 1 400 pounds and  for those on low pay clearly that's a  much higher percentage months ago yes  but at the time that that was that  decision was made the independent pay  review body was aware of rising  inflation that's why for example they  had a particular Target on for those on  Lower incomes now of course I would  dearly love to give nurses the 19 pay  rise that they would like as with the  the Prime Minister but the reason why  we're not able to do that is for two  reasons first of all the impact on  public finances if we gave just that  inflation across the board to everyone  that would cost households a thousand  pounds per household over the course of  the next year in addition to that and  those of your viewers with longer  memories will remember what happens if  we allow in payments to to get out of  control we do that does Stoke inflation  now what I want to do is make sure these  this initial economic stability that  we're restoring means we grow the  economy have a stronger economy and we  can afford higher pay Rises the  government is seeking to be reasonable  in its approach and I would urge all  unions to be reasonable and to to engage  rather than to strike those figures are  quite interesting Mr Dowden because it's  28 million households you're saying  it'll cost each household one thousand  pounds  um to meet this pay rise where did you  get those figures from because if we  look forward inflation is set to drop to  5.5 percent in 2023  um 2024. that figure could be from  around 400 to 500 pounds per household  so  are you misleading the public here well  if if the unions are are willing to  accept 5.5 percenters as you suggest  that's a that's a totally different  proposition and for a number of people  in the the public sector that is  essentially what this pay settlement  amounts to their demand is to have  inflation at the current level and for  some it's what's called RPI which  another measure inflation plus five  percent which takes you up to 19 so that  that's a totally different proposition  but the problem that we're seeing here  is that more nurses are leaving the  profession than coming into it and one  of the ways that you could secure a  future for nurses is pay  yes and we have given a a pay review a  pay rise determined by the independent  pay review body but that's not the only  thing we're doing we're recruiting a  large number of additional nurses we've  got a target of 50 000 we're halfway  along the path for that for example last  year the Royal College of Nursing asked  for bursaries of five thousand pounds or  in previous years they've asked for  bursaries of five thousand pounds not  repayable to help people get into the  profession we've given that I think  we've given support with training I  think up to about a thousand pounds so  we're taking a wide range of measures  it's not just in relation to pay  but the root out of this is pay  many nurses are saying we can't continue  in this job we can't heat our homes we  can't pay our bills and yet two years  ago we all clapped for the NHS and for  nurses isn't this the time to resolve it  before Christmas to ensure the safety  of the public  well I desperately want us to have a  resolution I would say to all unions to  call off these strikes and to give  people a break over Christmas give  families a break and give the the  military uh break in respect of of  nurses is precisely because of the value  that we hold them in that we broke the  overall freeze in public sectors  in addition and in uh addition to that  of course it is the case that people  will be able to get all sorts of other  supports for example there's 400 pounds  in relation to uh payments for energy  there's 150 pounds look how sustainable  is this at the moment though because the  chief of Defense staff is saying look  you these contingency plans that you've  brought in place to have 1 200 military  personnel Drive ambulances isn't enough  it doesn't go far enough are you worried  about the Christmas period and and also  if someone calls up uh Emergency  Services they dial 999 they've broken a  leg a pensioner has fallen over what  will happen in that time how long will  an ambulance take to get to them  well you've you've raised a number of  different points and I think it's  important that I try and address each of  those uh for the benefit of your viewers  now in relation to the ambulance strikes  we have expressed significant concern  about the impact that that will have and  we would urge ambulance strike leaders  to call it off even at this late stage  we are taking a range of contingencies  whether that's 600 military personnel  for driving 150 supporting in other  roles whether that's getting additional  clinicians into the 999 call centers to  make sure that people are dealt with on  the calls or whether that's getting  volunteer responders as well we are  working incredibly hard to make sure  particularly in the most  urgent cases people are able to get that  ambulance now the advice Remains the  case that if you have a serious  life-threatening uh incident that you  should still be able to call 999 if you  have uh lesser problems you should use  111 as before and you may seek to to use  a transport to get to a hospital  yourself the only way we can remove all  of this impact the only way is for this  to be called off this is not a situation  that we want to be in  so Mr Dalton how are talks going with  unions in particular the Royal College  of nurses how is it going where are we  at at the moment well remember the Royal  College of nurses doesn't relate to the  ambulance right that relates to the to  the license  and the the health secretary continues  to engage with with all unions relevant  to his sectors on on an ongoing basis  and that we we want to find a resolution  to this but the the house  are you hopeful there will be a  resolution of course I'm hoping that  there will be a resolution and I  desperately want I would love to be able  to sit here now and say to you and say  to all of your views and particularly  say to those nurses we will give you a  19 pay rise  and the reason why the reasons  if you allow me to finish my point  please the reason why that can't be done  is because of the costs the public  finances of an nineteen percent pay rise  if you think about across the private  sector many of the people listing public  and private sector are getting about  four percent that would be increased to  19 that would have a huge impact on  public finances it's not my money it's  not the government's money it's your  viewers money that is being spent we've  gone over here  I don't think it would be a reasonable  and that fig has been disputed of one  thousand pound every household  our viewers don't like to be misled  well I'm not misleading your viewers and  forgive me if you if you would please  not keep interrupting me I might be able  to get some of these points out in  relation to the value in relation we  want to hear from so they want to know  why a pay rise isn't possible and you're  telling our viewers that it would cost  them one thousand pounds and that's  simply been disputed  if you'll allow me to answer the please  go ahead  the the thousand pounds relates to if we  gave across the public sector an  inflation pay rise that would be a  thousand pounds for every household  that's 28 billion pounds in fact the  number would be higher if we matched the  RPI plus five percent that's the 19  which is being demanded in the RCN  dispute now as Government ministers we  have to balance up very difficult  choices we have to make sure the sums  are up for the public finances we have  to balance across what people are  getting in the wider private sector  which is about uh four percent and we  have to balance up the inflationary  impacts of this and the reason why and  the reason why we remit the reason why  we remember the reason why we remit this  to an independent pay-review body is  precisely to take the politics out of  this and it's not just me saying this  this is what for example the shadow  Chancellor for the labor party is also  said we to respect the independent pay  review processes I think that's a  reasonable approach to take the  government is being reasonable in  relation to this I completely understand  the pressures that people are on under  across the public and the private sector  for example with the the war in Ukraine  driving up the the cost of oil and  energy as we recover from the covid  crisis but I would say to union leaders  please give people a break this  Christmas we've got winter biting last  year we had the threat of lockdown  hanging over us things like the the  ambulance strike and the the rail strike  and not what families need this  Christmas Mr Dowden uh I don't think you  answered my question there the RNC have  said that they want to compromise are  you willing to meet them in the middle  here of course we will always in engage  we will continue to  engage uh with the RCN and uh with  others what I have sought to explain to  you and indeed to your viewers is to  understand why the government is taking  this approach we're trying to be  reasonable in relation to this however  we do have to balance off The Wider  public considerations in the in relation  to this this is what you would expect  ministers to do in this kind of scenario  and I have to say to to to the the union  leaders we will continue to be  reasonable but we also must be Resolute  in terms of protecting The Wider public  finances and that's a difficult argument  to make I accept it's a difficult  argument to make and I would dearly love  just to to for the government to be able  to write the check for a massive uh for  an increase in in public sector pay but  we've we've put it through this public  sector pay review bodies and we're  taking a reasonable and balanced  approach and I would urge the unions to  take a similarly reasonable approach  Oliver Dalton the chancellor of the  justee of Lancaster thank you very much  for joining us on Sky News today
 

